[Music]  south korea has gone its own way in the  fight against the coronavirus  testing was core to its strategy at the  start of the pandemic  people used masks in private homes  an app closely monitored the population  enabling authorities to track infection  chains  the vaccination campaign also quickly  picked up speed  now over 40 million people in south  korea have received at least one jab  that means over 80 percent of the  population is fully or partially  immunized among the highest vaccination  rates in the world  but has south korea done everything  right  and what can other countries learn  welcome i'm ben fazoulen is it time to  learn to just live with covert that's  what south korean officials are saying  experts around the world view the nation  as a role model in its handling of the  crisis  let's take a closer look  it's a big moment for manager lim young  man  he's opening this daytime disco in seoul  for the first time in six months  it comes as the government loosens many  of the restrictions imposed because of  the pandemic  like the patrons the owner is happy but  also nervous  i couldn't sleep well last night i was  worried but also i was excited because i  can run the business again what i'm  worried about is what if i have to close  again with more confirmed cases  but the authorities are taking steps to  prevent their new plan to live with  covid from leading to a new spike in  infections  for high-risk venues like bars and clubs  visitors have to show proof of  vaccination or a negative test  and while curbs on opening times for  cafes and restaurants will be dropped  there will be limits on the number of  unvaccinated people allowed into such  places  the authorities hope this will mark a  new beginning for the country  we're taking the first step today to  return to the path of everyday life  and it's a path we've never been on  the moves to lift restrictions are  possible because more than three  quarters of the population in south  korea are fully vaccinated  but the government stresses that the  efforts to contain covert must continue  people are being asked to keep on  wearing masks  and to ventilate indoor spaces  in this way they hope all curbs can be  lifted by next february  illahagli lectures in medical law and  ethics at yonsei university in a report  you wrote the government strategy  k-quarantine flattened the epidemic  curve without a complete lockdown  gaining attention worldwide but you said  the country had to pay dearly can you  tell us more about that  yes the government did not complete  banned  the whole society but actually it equals  to the complete band because the shop  sets close at nine o'clock nine o'clock  and the schools are around  one-third or half  and the younger generation lost their  chance to get a job  so people  suffered for the  listening of the  chance to their free lives  so  that's what happened in korea  so practically practically it was a  complete lockdown um but a lot of people  were taking it upon themselves uh to  take on that responsibility what i'd  like to know from you is if the  government is in return supporting them  financially  but the compensation was  uh  distributed quite late at the year and  it was not  the full compensation compared to other  european countries like uk or germany  so  the self-employed people got agitated  by the  fact that they are not completely or the  fully compensated by the government even  though they cooperate very actively with  the government policy well what's going  to happen now because a lot of those  businesses are saying they can't afford  to close again is it too early to lift  restrictions or or is this the only way  to deal with pandemic fatigue because  health officials say more people are  ignoring social distancing rules as this  pandemic fatigue sets in  oh there was a survey that about 40  percent of the self-employed person  seriously considered close up their  shops  because due to the loss of incomes and  heparins they have to pay  so  their agitation and their  loss of trust for the government  makes forced the government to lift the  ban or because the government  cannot say that  saving lives are everything because we  have to restore the society and also we  have to deal with this  injustice  uh what is inherent in the society i so  understand where people are coming from  because i've just been taking a look at  some of the laws this this cap on on  on unvaccinated people in cafes of up to  four unvaccinated people in a group  doesn't seem to make sense to me the the  100 people who can attend concerts and  musicals regardless of vaccination  status and then jim's no longer limiting  treadmill speeds because of the worry of  all that sweat i mean are these the  sorts of very specific rules that  only cause people to shake their heads  or worse still not even follow the rules  yeah because the government uh tried to  very specific  for  uh not to fully restrict the society  but uh in their attempt to  uh  not to close the society in the whole  society down but  the only possible way is make more  complicating laws  uh to deal with it but the self-employed  person and social society in general  is complaining  about the law the policies because it is  it looks very unreasonable and also  the application is not  fairly applied to every uh  to each of the every people group  and on top of that we've got winter  coming we've got delta around and cases  remain high although  this 80 vaccination rate seems like such  a success  why  is that not mirrored in the in the case  numbers  uh from the other phases korea has taken  the aggressive testing and treatment  policy i think this is can be one reason  of high numbers of the infected person  so far  16 million have been tested kobe 19  compared to south korean population is  48 million so one third of the korean  population in math  have been tested so the number could be  very high  and when i checked national statistics  about two-thirds of the patient are from  metrosol area which is one of the most  densely populated in the world  and also we need to pay attention to the  20s and 30s who constitute  33 of the compound cases they are most  active in the social relationships and  the least vaccinated growth so this  could explain the legality below  the the younger generation is  can explain the relatively low mortality  rate but  high  the impacted impaction rate  ilhackly thank you very much for your  insights and interesting talk coming to  us  from south korea there the medical law  and ethics division of yonsai university  thank you  time for one of your questions now  here's our science correspondent derek  williams  when will the novovax vaccine be rolled  out  novavax is an american biotech firm that  has developed a vaccine candidate which  produced promising results in  large-scale trials  those results  are currently being evaluated by medical  authorities in a number of places  including the eu  last week  indonesia became the first country to  approve the vaccine for emergency use  the company says that it expects other  countries to follow suit by the end of  the year  approval in indonesia though alone won't  be enough to secure approval from the  who which is  necessary if the novavax vaccine is to  play a role  in the kovacs initiative there it could  have an especially big impact  on the vaccine drive in poorer countries  because of how it's made the platform is  different than that used in vaccines  approved  so far in western countries they are  based on  information bearing molecules like  messenger rna which causes the  recipient's body  to produce copies of the viral spike  protein which ultimately leads to an  immune response against the virus  the novavax vaccine however contains  copies of the spike protein itself which  are produced in insect cells in  bioreactors  they draw the attention of the  recipient's immune cells setting a  cascade of events in motion  that includes the production of  antibodies against sars kobe 2 spikes  and hopefully  long-term immune memory of them one big  upside of the platform is that  the spike proteins are quite robust so  they don't need to be kept at ultra low  temperatures to remain stable  that makes the vaccine a good potential  candidate  for distribution in places where cold  chain issues  are a challenging factor  [Music]  and before we go a quick look at some of  the other covert stories making news  a 20-month u.s travel ban is over fully  vaccinated travelers from over 30  countries can now enter the united  states the european union lifted its  restrictions on american travelers back  in june  russians have returned to work after a  lockdown to contain the virus ended the  country reported nearly 40 000 new cases  on monday that's despite the closure of  most offices and public places since  october 28th  stay safe and see you again soon  [Music]  you

